d of the  story and anybody who pretends that  somehow biology determines everything is  really operating in bad faith so she  goes on and she says Humanity is  something more than a mere species it is  a historical development it is to be  defined by the manner in which it deals  with its natural fixed characteristics  its facticity  and that's a French term that we often  translate just as facticity and that's a  term that's particularly important  within existentialist ethics it means  the things that we're stuck with so for  example my appearance or my maleness or  my age in this case which I'm not  actually stuck with all that long  because I'm gonna be older later but I'm  never gonna be young again right unless  on there there's some sort of sci-fi  thing going on all of that is part of  our facticity and our facticity for an  existentialist does not determine us we  are working within the structures of  that facticity but it doesn't provide  you could say the absolute last word on  how things are going to go so she says  even with the most extreme bad faith in  the world it's impossible to demonstrate  the existence of a rivalry between the  human male and female of a truly  physiological nature so if we reject  that where do we go next because we do  seem to have this opposition and  long-term hostility going on she says  maybe their hostility could be allocated  to that intermediate terrain between  biology and psychology psychoanalysis  woman were told envies man his penis and  wishes to castrate him but the childish  desire for the penis is important in the  life of the adult woman only if she  feels her femininity as a mutilation  which Freud seemed to take for granted  but plenty of you know women and also  female psychoanalyst have come along and  said  maybe you're wrong about that part of  the theory maybe you're extrapolating  too much here and so that's being  rejected as well she says that you know  maybe it's just a symbol of all the  privileges